gem:description a owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf dc:description ;
    gem:prefLabel "Description" ;
    gem:description "A 'Description' is a written account that provides details about an ontological term, aimed at creating a vivid mental image for the reader. It acts as a representation of characteristics, often functioning to explain what something is, its appearance, or its nature." ;
    gem:llmDetailedDefinition "A 'Description' is an annotation that provides a concise human-readable explanation of what a schema element or instance represents. On classes, it defines the class's scope. On properties, it explains the relationship's meaning. On instances, it provides narrative detail about the entity — for events, this is a paragraph-length account of what happened. The gem:description on instances is typically the richest source of factual content in the ontology and should be the primary source for answering questions about what happened, who was involved, and why." ;
    gem:memberOfOntology gem:gemOntology .

#
#  The following diagnosis code attributes replicate the current
#  "relational/object" list of attributes in the XOM object model.
#  There are features available in graph databases that provide
#  better extensibility and performance: attribute lists and linked
#  data.
#
#  Attribute lists employ a single attribute definition, but 
#  support any number of instances of the attribute, and do not
#  require the definition of a list size in the data model.  Any
#  number of attributes employing the same definition can be added
#  to a BaseServiceLine instance, subject to any data quality rules
#  for a minimum and/or maximum number of entries in the list.
#
#  Linked data stores no attributes at all in the BaseServiceLine 
#  instance for data that is represented in another class.  A link
#  would be created from the BaseServiceLine instance to the ICD10
#  instance that represents the diagnosis code employed.  Like in
#  attribute lists, an unlimited number of links can be employed,
#  subject to any rules for a cardinality minimum and/or maximum.
#
#  Links on RDF graphs reduce space requirements in the instance and
#  offer equivalent performance to attributes associated with the
#  class instance.  Links on Neo4j graphs both reduce space 
#  requirements in the instance and offers faster query performance
#  than storing the attributes in the Neo4j node instance.  "Fat
#  nodes" in Neo4j suffer from attribute search performance
#  degradation, which affects query performance.
#  
#
gem:diagnosisCode1 a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    gem:prefLabel "Diagnosis Code 1" ;
    gem:description "A 'Diagnosis Code 1' is a string datatype property that contains the first secondary ICD-10-CM diagnosis code reported on a Medicare claim, alongside the primary diagnosis." ;
    gem:llmScopingNote """Secondary ICD-10-CM diagnosis slot 1 on a claim (raw STRING), alongside gem:primaryDiagnosisCode; part of the positional array gem:diagnosisCode1..12. Object-link form is gem:hasICDdiagnosis (range gem:ICDdiagnosis). Claim/service-line runtime field; unpopulated in the current coverage-policy instance graph (see gem:MedicareClaim).""" ;
    rdfs:domain gem:BaseServiceLine ;
    rdfs:range  xsd:string ;
    gem:sourceAttribute "diagCd1" ;
    gem:memberOfOntology gem:gemOntology .

